1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
So, you know, it's not the act of making a podcast that makes you crazy.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Podcasting doesn't make you crazy.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, it just makes your craziness a little more accessible to the rest of the world.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
How about that?

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Hey, this is podcast number 81.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Music

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Hey, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
This is Clark Green. I'm back.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
How about that?

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Yeah. We had a big trip and about three weeks of traveling and scouting.

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Guess where?

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
There's a little musical hint.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,000
You got it yet?

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,000
That's right.

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Yes, we were in the wonderful country of Switzerland

17
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
at Kondersteg International Scout Center for a week with a group of our Scouts.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
And what a wonderful experience.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
That was preceded by a week of vacation with my lovely wife in the same town in Kondersteg.

20
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And that was preceded by a week at summer camp.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
So basically, I've been pretty much out of circulation for the past three or four weeks

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
in between all of the traveling and scouting that we got to do.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
And I'm just so happy to be back home.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Well, you know, pretty happy to be back home.

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
I had to leave some pretty marvelous places behind.

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
But home is a pretty marvelous place, too.

27
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
First of all, let's see.

28
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Now that we're back, we got a lot of cleaning up to do.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
First, a couple of abject apologies.

30
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,000
That's right.

31
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
I had some podcasts and posts and things like that ready to go out

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
and programmed to go out over the time I was gone.

33
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
The posts did pretty well.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
The podcast, oh my, I put a couple of phantom podcasts out.

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
What I had hoped to be able to do was in the time that I was traveling,

36
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
be able to, you know, put together a couple of quick podcasts for you.

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
And I had things set up.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Well, it didn't work out.

39
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
And so there's been a number of you have been writing in about the fact

40
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
that the podcast seems to have disappeared from iTunes

41
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
and that you couldn't download the podcast.

42
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And I really apologize for all the frustration.

43
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
But like I said, you know, I was away.

44
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I didn't have web access.

45
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And actually to tell you the God's honest truth,

46
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
I wasn't all that concerned because, man,

47
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
if you've never been to Switzerland, wow, what a place.

48
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Just an incredible country with...

49
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
We were in the heart of the Bernese Alps.

50
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
And, you know, you just kind of wake up in a state of slack-jawed awe

51
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
every day and you get to do some marvelous hikes

52
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
and meet some wonderful people.

53
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Kondersteg International Scout Center has been operating since the 1920s

54
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and it is a center for scouts from all over the world

55
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
who come and visit during the summer and all throughout the year,

56
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
but mainly during the summer.

57
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And just to participate in some scout brotherhood

58
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
and to take in the wonderful sights around the town of Kondersteg.

59
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
We met scouts from the United Kingdom.

60
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
We met scouts from Russia.

61
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
How about that?

62
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Russia.

63
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
We met scouts from Portugal, from Ireland,

64
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
from an incredible number of countries all around the world.

65
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Brazil.

66
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
And what a wonderful, wonderful time.

67
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
So here's a couple of quick takeaways for you.

68
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Scouting is still a relevant, wonderful worldwide movement

69
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
and the people that you get to meet are just wonderful folks.

70
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
We all share much more in common than we do in the way of differences.

71
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
So the world is sometimes a troubled and difficult place,

72
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
but as long as there are scouts in it,

73
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
there's going to be that bright point of light out there.

74
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
The other big takeaway that I'll repeat at Infinatum

75
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
is that if you set your mind to it,

76
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
if your scouts get excited and motivated about doing something,

77
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
anything from a big international trip like this,

78
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
just to, you know, a weekend camping trip, you can do it.

79
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,000
It can happen.

80
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
And it also takes us a little vision,

81
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
a little leadership on the part of your scouts,

82
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,000
and it will happen.

83
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
And I don't normally call out my scouts by name

84
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
or refer to them personally,

85
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:07,000
but I've got to tell you,

86
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
the crew chief that we selected for this particular trip,

87
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Robert H.,

88
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
and is just a wonderful leader,

89
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
and he put a lot of energy into it,

90
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
and I think he had the best time of all the scouts

91
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
that attended that trip.

92
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
So kudos to you, Robert H.,

93
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
for having done a wonderful job in making that trip happen

94
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
for your fellow scouts and for us adults, too.

95
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
So, I'll have much more, probably more than you want to hear

96
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
to say about that whole thing in future podcasts

97
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and blog posts and things,

98
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
but let's get down to brass tacks.

99
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
I've got interviews lined up for this fall

100
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
with authors and things.

101
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
I've got gear reviews,

102
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and hopefully within the next week or so,

103
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
we'll see the return of our weekly caption contest

104
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
on Tuesdays.

105
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
I got lots of nice messages of welcome home.

106
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Thanks, folks.

107
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And oh, I should mention,

108
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,000
I have an interview in the works

109
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
about the Journey to Excellence program.

110
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Now, if you're not familiar with the Journey to Excellence program

111
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
or you haven't heard of it,

112
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
well, get on the BSA national website,

113
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
search for Journey to Excellence,

114
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
and you'll see that it is the new unit evaluation program

115
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
that promises to actually be one of

116
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
a very revolutionary tool

117
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
for scout leaders to use

118
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
to work on their unit effectiveness.

119
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
I have interview in the works with one of the volunteers

120
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
who developed this program at a national level,

121
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
and so what I would like to do

122
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
in preparation for that interview

123
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
is hear your questions or comments

124
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
about the Journey to Excellence program.

125
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
I can pass those questions and comments onto him

126
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
and see what we can learn.

127
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
I heard from Kent Jennings,

128
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,000
who is from South Africa.

129
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
He says, I'm a member of the Orion rover crew

130
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
and one of our rovers attended,

131
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Kent Jennings.

132
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
Great to see you advertising, scouting.

133
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
I enjoy your blog.

134
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
It's great to see others who are willing to write.

135
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Yours and scouting, Kent Jennings.

136
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Well, thanks, Kent.

137
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
I did not run into anybody from South Africa

138
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
while I was at Kent Jennings,

139
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
at least not that I can remember,

140
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
but I certainly appreciate you being in touch.

141
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Thundering, Thor got back in touch.

142
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
He said, thank you for giving the National Park Service programs

143
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
for scouts some well-deserved publicity.

144
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
And if you missed that, it's a blog post.

145
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
There are national park service programs

146
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
that recognize scouts and give you some activities

147
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
that you can do while visiting national parks.

148
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Thor goes on to say it's a wonderful program

149
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
not only for Boy Scouts,

150
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
but for Cub Scouts and Girl Scouts of all levels.

151
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
The National Park Service Rangers and personnel

152
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
have been helpful and always happy

153
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
to lend a hand with our scouts.

154
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
The Junior Ranger programs,

155
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
available at each national park service site,

156
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
also make for lots of fun.

157
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Thanks again for all you do.

158
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
And PS, the Green Bar Bill podcast was awesome.

159
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Well, thank you for being in touch again, Thor.

160
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
It's always good to hear from everybody.

161
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
So we're going at light with this podcast.

162
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
There's a lot of threads to pick up

163
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and things to take care of,

164
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
you know, as well as keeping up with the blog

165
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and the podcast and the scout troop.

166
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
So you're going to have to be patient with me.

167
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
I did have a recent discussion about discipline

168
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
in scouting that I think be useful to talk about

169
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,000
for a little bit.

170
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
And we're going to do that in scout master's ship

171
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
in seven minutes or less,

172
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
which is the biggest fib going

173
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
as far as the podcast is concerned.

174
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
I think we'll probably go over seven minutes,

175
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,000
but hey.

176
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
And that's going to do it for the podcast this time.

177
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,000
So let's get started, shall we?

178
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Scout master's ship in seven minutes or less.

179
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
This coming April marks something over 30 years

180
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
as a scout leader, as an adult volunteer.

181
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
And it's been a wonderful ride.

182
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
But there are always challenges

183
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and there are difficulties

184
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and some of these surround discipline in scouting.

185
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
I mean, we're dealing with boys

186
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
at a very formative part

187
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
during a very formative part of their lives

188
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
who are pushing limits and trying and testing

189
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
and doing things.

190
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And we do have to have some kind of discipline

191
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
and we have to be able to exercise discipline

192
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
and to address situations as they arise.

193
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
This is not, you know,

194
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
one of the fun parts of being a scout leader.

195
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
It can be very daunting and frustrating

196
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:46,000
and difficult

197
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and discouraging.

198
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
But if we're prepared ahead of time,

199
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
like most things,

200
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
we're going to be able to meet these challenges head-on

201
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
and to dispatch them in a way

202
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
that is both positive for our scouts

203
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and is not too trying for ourselves.

204
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
This subject came up

205
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
in a little back-and-forth discussion I had

206
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
via Facebook

207
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and somebody had asked me a couple of questions

208
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
about a discipline problem they were having

209
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and we got to talking back-and-forth

210
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
and I want to take a moment

211
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
to treat the idea of discipline

212
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
and scouting with some broad underlying concepts.

213
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
And if you're familiar with the podcast,

214
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
that's one of my favorite phrases,

215
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
broad underlying concepts.

216
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
I can give you some specifics

217
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and some suggestions,

218
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
but what you really need

219
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
is you need to be grounded in the

220
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
broad underlying concepts

221
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
that will help you dispense

222
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
and deal with and

223
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
make discipline

224
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
in your scout unit

225
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
a viable

226
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and useful and positive,

227
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
positive thing.

228
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I read a recent posting

229
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
at Ask Andy

230
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and Andy is a scout commissioner

231
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
who has kind of an advice column

232
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
at the United States Scouts

233
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Service Project website.

234
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Very easy to find.

235
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Just Google Ask Andy

236
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and Scouts

237
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and you'll find his columns

238
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
and there's several years of them there,

239
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
but he had a couple of statements

240
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
that I think are very useful,

241
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
so my tip of the campaign had to Andy.

242
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
He said this,

243
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
in the BSA adult unit level

244
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
volunteers

245
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
may guide scouts in establishing

246
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
a troop culture

247
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
of order and correct behavior.

248
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
However,

249
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
punishment in any form

250
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
is not a part of the scouting experience.

251
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
The absence of overt punishment

252
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
does not mean suggest or imply

253
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
that actions have no consequences.

254
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
All actions have consequences.

255
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
They're commensurate with the nature

256
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
of the action.

257
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
The scout master is ultimately responsible

258
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
for the scout's behavior and comportment.

259
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
If the scout master is unsuccessful

260
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
in finding the best in a boy

261
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and bringing it out,

262
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
then he can ask for help.

263
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
If the scout master is unable

264
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
to understand the current behavior,

265
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
they ought to be replaced with someone

266
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
who can fulfill the responsibilities

267
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
of the position.

268
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
That kind of helps identify

269
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
some of the concepts

270
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
of discipline in scouting.

271
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
I'm going to keep hammering

272
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
on one key statement.

273
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Punishment

274
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
is just not a part

275
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
of the scouting experience.

276
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Some of you may not like

277
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
that particular statement

278
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and may not think that you can

279
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
maintain discipline without the threat

280
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
of punishment, but you can.

281
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
And we're going to talk about how.

282
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
We want to remember that discipline

283
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
means to teach.

284
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
It has similar roots

285
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
to the word disciple.

286
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Discipline does not mean

287
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
exclusively to punish.

288
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Effective discipline is consistent

289
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
and requires patience.

290
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Effective discipline doesn't stifle

291
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
scouts. It gives them

292
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
dependable foundation

293
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
from which to grow and mature.

294
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Because we have to remember what their job is.

295
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
You're dealing with boys

296
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
if you're in a scout troop

297
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
who are between the ages of 10 and a half

298
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and 17.

299
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Part of their job at this age

300
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
is to find boundaries.

301
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
The way they find boundaries

302
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
is they walk right up to them

303
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
and then they jump over them.

304
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
They test you

305
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and they're not testing you personally

306
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
they're seeing if the world

307
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
is going to live up to these promises.

308
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And because they're not

309
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
testing you personally

310
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
it's important that

311
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
areas of discipline

312
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
be divorced from your

313
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
personal emotional reactions.

314
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
I mean you're going to have some

315
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
predictable emotional reactions.

316
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
You're going to get angry.

317
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
You're going to get frustrated.

318
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Oftentimes you'll be embarrassed.

319
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
You'll be sad.

320
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
You'll be disappointed.

321
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
But these are not very helpful

322
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
emotions when it comes to discipline.

323
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Now if you're listening

324
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
to this podcast and you're a volunteer

325
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
scout leader, chances are

326
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
very good chances are

327
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
that you're also a parent.

328
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
And when I say that your child's

329
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
testing of boundaries

330
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and things like that is not personal

331
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
you may not believe me.

332
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Because they know how to make it

333
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
personal don't they?

334
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
And it can be very very harmful.

335
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
It can be really really upsetting.

336
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
The thing is that as a parent

337
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
and as a volunteer

338
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
scout leader and having observed

339
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
all of these processes for a good many years

340
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
there ain't nothing special about

341
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
your boy or your daughter

342
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
because all of them

343
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
all of them

344
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
do this. It's part of their job.

345
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
And

346
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
I think understanding that

347
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
helps us divorce

348
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
our reaction

349
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
to what they do

350
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
from our emotions.

351
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
We need to connect actions

352
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
with consequences.

353
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
That's important.

354
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
There are two types

355
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
of consequences to any action.

356
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
One is natural

357
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
and one

358
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
is logical.

359
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
Natural consequences are automatic

360
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and they really don't require our intervention.

361
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
I'll give you a simple example.

362
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
If I was told

363
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
to bring rain gear with me

364
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
on a camping trip

365
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
and I don't bring it and it rains and I get wet

366
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
that's a natural consequence.

367
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
If somebody says to me

368
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
do not touch that it is hot

369
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
and I touch it and I get burned

370
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
that's a natural consequence.

371
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
There are also

372
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
logical consequences

373
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
and those logical consequences

374
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
are something that we may impose

375
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and you may think

376
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
that I am trying to avoid

377
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
the word punish

378
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
but I'm not trying to avoid it because

379
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
what I'm talking about

380
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
is not aimed at

381
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
punishing behavior.

382
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
It's aimed at correcting it.

383
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
So let's talk about that

384
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
for a moment. It's not aimed

385
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
at punishing behavior.

386
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
It's aimed at correcting it.

387
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
So part of discipline is about

388
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
correcting wrong behavior

389
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
but part of discipline is also

390
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
reinforcing good actions.

391
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Whenever it's possible as scout leaders

392
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
I think we need to rely on

393
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
natural consequences

394
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
and we let scouts deal with the fallout of their actions.

395
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
We don't rescue them

396
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
when it's not an issue

397
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
of physical safety or propriety.

398
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
We don't rescue them.

399
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
If they make

400
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
bad plans

401
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
and it's not going to threaten their health or safety

402
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
we should let them

403
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
deal with the fallout

404
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and do it in a very

405
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
understanding way

406
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
and not lord it over them

407
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and tell them I told you so

408
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
but you know just say hey

409
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
been there this is really frustrating

410
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
isn't it? You didn't plan very well

411
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and now you're really frustrated.

412
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
So what's our way out of the situation?

413
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
In the best case scenario

414
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
natural consequences run their course

415
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
without much intervention from adult leadership at all.

416
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
Other than

417
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
to you know reflect and

418
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
discuss on the situation and see if we can turn it into

419
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
some kind of a teachable moment.

420
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
When the actions of

421
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
scouts create an immediate threat to health or safety

422
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
those actions have to be stopped immediately.

423
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
And then

424
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
scouts need to

425
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
work with the logical consequences

426
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
of their bad actions.

427
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Now the discussion

428
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
that spurred this part

429
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
of the podcast

430
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
surrounded the improper use of matches

431
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
on camping trips.

432
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
I mean what would you do if you found

433
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
freshly burnt matches and match books in the area

434
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
you were camping?

435
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
And this is obviously a situation

436
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
where we can't allow the natural

437
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
consequences of the actions to do the job.

438
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Because somebody

439
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
or something

440
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
could get burned and that's really just

441
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
not an option.

442
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
Step one

443
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
is to take this issue

444
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
to the patrol leaders council

445
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and ask them what the natural

446
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
consequences of sex actions would be.

447
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Tell us

448
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
we found these matches

449
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
match books. Somebody's playing with matches.

450
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Somebody's not using them responsibly.

451
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
You know

452
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
tell me what

453
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
the possibilities are here.

454
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Tell me what could happen if we allow this

455
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
to go on.

456
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
And you know they're

457
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
going to think about it a little bit.

458
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
They're going to come up with well somebody could set

459
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
fire. Somebody could burn themselves.

460
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Somebody could threaten everyone's safety with

461
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
this. And you know these are

462
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
fairly self-evident things but you don't

463
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
want to preach at your patrol

464
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
leaders council. What you want to do

465
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
is you want to poke at them with some questions

466
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
and get them to identify things

467
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
as self-evident as they might be.

468
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
And you want them to be able

469
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
to put these things into words.

470
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Ask them what they think logical

471
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
consequences of the actions should be.

472
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
And remember that punishment

473
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
is not an option.

474
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
Now I've been all around the block

475
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
with the whole punishment thing

476
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
because our natural reaction

477
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
our natural reaction

478
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
to bad actions

479
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
is to default to this

480
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
punishment model.

481
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
You know

482
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
well hey you didn't wear your neck a chip

483
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
giving me 10 push ups.

484
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
You didn't do thus and so

485
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
or you did thus and so so now you're going to be

486
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
grounded or you're going to be denied privileges

487
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
and you're going to be

488
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
dismissed from the troop or you're going to be

489
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
suspended or

490
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
there's all of these elaborate

491
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
plans that we draw up and things

492
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
that we think through for stuff like that

493
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
but I want you to try

494
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
thinking about discipline

495
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
absent

496
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
the option of punishing.

497
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Does your patrol leaders council

498
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
need to discover

499
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
who the individual actor was

500
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
in this situation?

501
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Do they need to figure out who was playing

502
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
with matches?

503
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
This is a pretty important question

504
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
because how is this going to play out

505
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and is it really going to be all that useful?

506
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
How are they going to make that happen?

507
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
Coercing a confession

508
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
with the threat of punishment

509
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
is not an option for us

510
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
in scouting and I got to tell you

511
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
it doesn't really work.

512
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Our gut reaction is usually

513
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
identifying and punishing an individual

514
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
for some kind of bad action.

515
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
We do this out of a sense of fairness

516
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
but more often than not

517
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
that sense of fairness

518
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
gets misplaced.

519
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
We can also do this out of a sense

520
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
of frustration and anger

521
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and we have to be careful about that.

522
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Scouting is a good opportunity

523
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
for all of us to learn that

524
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
the failing of an individual

525
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
is often now

526
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
not always but often the failure

527
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
of the group to support and inform them.

528
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
I got to tell you in a situation like this

529
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
and many associated situations

530
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
if the bad actor doesn't immediately

531
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
identify themselves

532
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
locating the individual

533
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and getting them to fess up

534
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
can be hugely counterproductive.

535
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
I've

536
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
tried it.

537
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
I've tried it. It doesn't

538
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
work very well.

539
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
I refuse to be an investigator

540
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and I refuse to be a policeman.

541
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
I don't have the training skill

542
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
natural ability for any of that.

543
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
I'm not going to try to interview

544
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
witnesses and try and figure things out.

545
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
I really don't have

546
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
that ability.

547
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
When we attempt to identify

548
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
an individual bad actor everything usually

549
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
comes to a stop. We find the matches

550
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
we find the burnt match books

551
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
whatever. Get the troop together

552
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
we say okay fellas there's a problem

553
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
here somebody's been lighting matches

554
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
and I need to know who it is

555
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
and we're going to stand

556
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
right here until somebody

557
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
tells me who it is

558
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
well now you've

559
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
so stigmatized that

560
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
that

561
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
you've created some real disincentive

562
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
for the person to come forward

563
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
because they're not going to come forward

564
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
you're going to get even more frustrated

565
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
and a little more angry and then you might hey

566
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
if we can't make this happen right now

567
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
if somebody doesn't step up and tell me who it is

568
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
we're going to pack up everything and we're going to go home

569
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
I've been there before

570
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
you know

571
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
let me let me take a minute I don't want to get too

572
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
diverted from the subject but let me take a minute here

573
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
I'm not trying to judge you

574
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
I'm not trying to judge your actions your feelings

575
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
the way that you do things the way that you raise your children

576
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
what I'm trying to do

577
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
is point out some of the mistakes

578
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
that I've made along the way

579
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
if this connects with you that's fine

580
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
you know I'm not trying to make out

581
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
that I am the

582
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
all wise

583
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
wizard here because I'm not

584
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
I've made these mistakes

585
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
and I still make them

586
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
if we get that group of scouts together and we try

587
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
and coerce a confession out of an individual

588
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
you can see what's happening right I'm up in the ante

589
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
hey we're going to pack up and go home

590
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
if nobody steps forward

591
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
I've had it I'm not dealing with you guys anymore

592
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
that could in a very small percentage

593
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
of instances maybe

594
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
get somebody to step forward but that

595
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
generally is not going to happen you're going to pack up and go home

596
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
and now you've

597
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
up the ante even more

598
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
because you've packed up and gone home so the next time

599
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
we get together with scouts and this has already gone through

600
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
the troop committee and all the parents have discussed it

601
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
and the scouts have discussed it

602
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
you're going to say we need to find out who

603
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
lit those matches that scouts not coming

604
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
forward now because

605
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
the ante is too high

606
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
the price to pay is too high

607
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
now if we approach this in a different way

608
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
if we say someone playing

609
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
with matches means that we haven't done a very good

610
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
job as a troop and a patrol in teaching safety

611
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
ask

612
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
the patrol leaders council

613
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
what they think a logical consequence of that should be

614
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
maybe they should voluntarily

615
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
forgo all fire lighting privileges

616
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
for a given time for everybody in the whole troop

617
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
that doesn't seem fair

618
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
but if you think about it

619
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
this connects

620
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
all the individuals together

621
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
hey these are important things this is an important thing

622
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
and we have to obey

623
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
the rules and regs around this

624
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
and if one of us isn't doing it

625
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
that could mean that

626
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
we're in some trouble here

627
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
perhaps the patrol leaders council

628
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
will plan

629
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
a fire safety session for the next meeting

630
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
for everyone

631
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
if you encourage group responsibility

632
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
you're going to get responsible scouts individually

633
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
and as a group

634
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
if you punish individuals

635
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
you'll end up more

636
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
with this kind of every man for himself mentality

637
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
than a sense of cooperative responsibility

638
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
and

639
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
progressively more challenging and difficult things we do

640
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
in the outdoors

641
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
you've got to have cooperative responsibility

642
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
individual responsibility

643
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
absolutely important

644
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
absolutely has to be

645
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
an aspect

646
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
of what we do in scouting

647
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
but cooperative group responsibility

648
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
also

649
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
incredibly important

650
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
and they need to be able to tie those two concepts together

651
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
I can't be all taken care of

652
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
and set up and

653
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
safe and dry

654
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
and watch as my fellow scout

655
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
is out there wet and unsafe

656
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
and not doing well

657
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
because we all

658
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
need to stick together

659
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
I think when individuals are punished or sanctioned

660
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
it has

661
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
a smaller chance of helping them

662
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
learn anything other than shame

663
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
other than feeling ashamed

664
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
and

665
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
you know sometimes in a way that

666
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
is kind of irreparable

667
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
it also makes the results of admitting

668
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
fault

669
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
so unpleasant

670
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
that we end up placing the wrongdoer on one side

671
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
and the rest of their friends

672
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
and scouting and the adults and the committee

673
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
and all the parents on the other

674
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
and I just don't think that that is

675
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
really very helpful

676
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
and when we're talking about individuals

677
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
we've got to

678
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
I'm going to go back to something that

679
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
Andy wrote

680
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
in the same column

681
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
he says

682
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
yes a scout may do a bad thing

683
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
that's behavior

684
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
but when we call him

685
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
a liar if he lied

686
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
or we call him a fire bug if he lit matches

687
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
we're cutting to his very nature

688
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
and branding his character

689
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
rather than dealing

690
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
with the inappropriate behavior

691
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
I'll add to that

692
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
that we need to once again be aware

693
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
of our emotional reactions

694
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
and that we don't let it

695
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
inform our actions

696
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
and keep upping the ante

697
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
on these individuals

698
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
now what I've described is

699
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
you know

700
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
certainly a serious

701
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
problem

702
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
and one way of looking at it

703
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
it's a reasonably serious problem with the matches

704
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
and you know

705
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
that could turn into something very serious

706
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
indeed

707
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
it's not necessarily

708
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
you know the top tier

709
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
difficulties and serious problems that I've had

710
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
as a scout leader

711
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
I have

712
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
rarely had to deal with the logical consequences

713
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
of an individual's behavior

714
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
in scouting

715
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
that resulted in them having to deal with the police

716
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
like I say

717
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
not to tempt fate, knocked wood

718
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
that I haven't had to deal with that very much

719
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
basically twice

720
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
in the 30 years of working as a scout leader

721
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
both times the offenses

722
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
were only kind of tangentially related

723
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
to scouting activities

724
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
they didn't occur

725
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
on a troop outing or at a meeting

726
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
they occurred outside of those things

727
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
now when I was made aware of the incidents

728
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
and the charges

729
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
I did have to take some action

730
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
I mean I couldn't ignore them

731
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
because in both cases

732
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
the actions of the individual scouts

733
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
resulted in charges that could have been

734
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
detrimental to our scouts

735
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
if they were repeated during an outing or a meeting

736
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
I can tell you that

737
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
one was assault related

738
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
and one was drug related

739
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
it's a serious problem for a young man

740
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
and in those two instances

741
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
you know we had to look at that

742
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and say well

743
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
until this gets resolved

744
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
we're going to have to ask you to stay away

745
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
from scouts for the time being

746
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
it's not that we think you're guilty

747
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
it's not that we think you're innocent

748
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
it's not that we really think anything about you at all

749
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
but you've gotten yourself into a bit of a pickle here

750
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
and until it's resolved

751
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
by the authorities

752
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
there's really not a whole lot we're going to be able to do

753
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
in both cases after consulting with the troop committee

754
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
chair

755
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
the scout was suspended from activities with the troop

756
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
until everything was resolved

757
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
by the authorities

758
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
and then the

759
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
next step would be the scout to meet

760
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
with the troop committee and map a way forward

761
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
if he was going to continue in scouting

762
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
one of them did

763
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
and one of them hasn't so far

764
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
doors still open

765
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
our overall goal in dealing with these incidents

766
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
was to preserve the safety of our scouts

767
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
the privacy

768
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
of the offending scout

769
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
and his family

770
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
and to give him

771
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
a road back to redeem themselves

772
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
after the authorities have made their determinations

773
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
more routine discipline problems

774
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
get dealt with on a case to case basis

775
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
we don't have a policy or a plan that describes

776
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
offenses and consequences

777
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
we're informed

778
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
only by the scout oath and law

779
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
that is

780
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
the gold standard for behavior

781
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
and discipline

782
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
I gotta say if you have such a written policy

783
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
that describes

784
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
punishments

785
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
that

786
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
has this kind of tiered approach

787
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
to discipline problems

788
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
that includes you know well you're going to have a conference with the scout master

789
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
then the senior patroller

790
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
blah blah blah

791
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
that might be helpful

792
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
in some respects but I gotta tell you

793
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
consider

794
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
every serious discipline problem is unique

795
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
because it's coming from an individual

796
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
and it will ultimately need to be addressed

797
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,000
on an individual basis

798
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
if you're hemmed in by a policy statement

799
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,000
that you've made

800
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:06,000
you may not have the latitude to act

801
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,000
in the best interest of everybody involved

802
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,000
just a little bit of advice there

803
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,000
this is a big subject

804
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:14,000
it's a tough subject

805
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
and like I say there's lots of emotions

806
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
and difficulties and questions

807
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
and morality and things involved in it

808
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:22,000
but just to review

809
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
when we encounter

810
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:26,000
discipline problems in scouting

811
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
we need to

812
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,000
divorce our emotional reaction from it

813
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
we need to understand that discipline

814
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
is not punishment

815
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,000
discipline is teaching

816
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
we can turn these difficulties

817
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
most of the time

818
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
not always but most of the time

819
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,000
we can turn them into something positive

820
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
and something that is instead of a tool

821
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
for beating somebody down

822
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
for building them up

823
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:52,000
instead of an incident

824
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
that's going to sap the morale

825
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:56,000
away from our troop

826
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
we can build it into something

827
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
that bolsters

828
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,000
and broadens the morale of the troop

829
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,000
we can

830
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:06,000
save individuals from

831
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,000
being stigmatized and castigated

832
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:10,000
and cast out of

833
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:12,000
their circle of friends sometimes

834
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,000
for mistakes

835
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,000
that they are going to make

836
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:18,000
they're all going to make them

837
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,000
it's going to be your child one day

838
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:22,000
it's going to happen

839
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,000
so this is

840
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
a pretty broad discussion

841
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
broad underlying concepts

842
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
I would love to hear your thoughts

843
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
and reflections on the subject

844
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
and I'll tell you how to get in touch with me

845
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
in just a moment

846
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:46,000
music

847
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
well thanks for listening

848
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,000
it sure is good to be back

849
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,000
and to be putting the Scoutmaster podcast together again

850
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
here's how you can keep in touch

851
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
you can read the Scoutmaster blog

